// A synchronous call: free within a container, middleware across (F3). At an effect site a
// synchronous step in its own transaction (the step form, AB37).
sync : SyncCall { }

// Fire and forget through the broker: an event (F5), or a saga step when the cut separates classes
// written in one transaction (F6).
async : AsyncCall { }

// In the caller's transaction (DESIGN.md AB37): at an effect site whose caller and provider share
// a container and whose two schemas share a database server, the provider's work runs as a local
// call in the caller's transaction (CallSite.joinedService: no envelope, no commit of its own, no
// message); otherwise a synchronous step. Allowed at every internal effect site, not at the
// provider callbacks.
inTx : InTransaction { }

// A read-through cache in the caller: hitRatio 0.8 (assumed), hitCpuMs 0 (the platform's: the
// caller's CPU per hit net of the saved call's client work, at least 0; campaign v4,
// cases/misarch/timing/parts.json mechanisms.parameters callerCacheHitCpuMs -0.591 ms, the hit
// saves the caller CPU, RESULTS §5; AB64).
cache : CachedCall {
    hitRatio = 0.8
    hitCpuMs = 0.0
}

// A gateway join (federation): across containers the gateway fetches the field after the request's
// root resolver, gatewayFetchCpuMs 1.099; within one container the resolver resolves it itself
// (F5).
join : ComposedCall {
    composeCpuMs = 1.099
}

// A gateway join with a cache, as built (DESIGN.md AB33.1): the provider's entity cache behind its
// sidecar. The gateway fetches the field (composeCpuMs 1.099) through the middleware either way; a
// hit costs the provider hitCpuMs 0.713 (campaign v4, cases/misarch/timing/RESULTS.md §5, no
// database; measured at shipment and catalog: in the model a hit is the lookup, 0 ms (ESTIMATE:
// the measured hit minus those providers' mean receiving envelope, at least 0), in the cached
// operation and the provider's receiving envelope after the fetch, AB56, AB64), a miss missCpuMs 0
// more and the field's operation. The hit ratio is the site's (CallSite.hitRatio; 0.8 where a site
// has none); the cache sits in each site's cachedService.
joinCache : ComposedCall {
    composeCpuMs = 1.099
    hitRatio     = 0.8
    hitCpuMs     = 0.713
    missCpuMs    = 0.0
}

// A local copy of the accessed class in the consumer (F4): readDbMs 0.141 (per copy read of one
// statement, measured), readCpuMs 0.1467 (the reader's CPU per copy read, approximate), writeCpuMs
// 1.083 (per delivered copy event, approximate), writeDbMs 0.321 (measured); campaign v4,
// cases/misarch/timing/RESULTS.md §5 (AB32, AB64). Across containers a copy read is stale and
// couples like a gateway join.
copy : LocalCopy {
    readDbMs       = 0.141
    writeCpuMs     = 1.083
    writeDbMs      = 0.321
    readCpuMs      = 0.1467
    couplingWeight = 0.5
    staleReads     = 1.0
}

// A saga as a choreography: every step made by its own component.
choreography : Choreographed { }

// A saga orchestrated by its trigger's component: orchestratorStepCpuMs 0.799 per step (derived,
// not measured: MiSArch has no orchestrator; per step the coordinator persists the saga state, one
// upsert handler without its inbound part, campaign v4, cases/misarch/timing/parts.json
// mechanisms.parameters replicaWriteCpuMs.extraUpsertOnly; the messages are the middleware blocks,
// AB33.7, AB64).
orchestration : Orchestrated {
    stepCpuMs = 0.799
}

// Middleware costs of round 2 (msa System, calibrated at load): a hop 0.766 ms on the caller's
// sidecar, 0.2 ms latency, 1.095 ms on the callee's; a message publishCpuMs 0.434, the broker,
// deliverCpuMs 1.1. Database servers are reached directly by their class's component (F8). Kept
// with the campaign's timing (AB32.3, AB64): the campaign v4 measured 1.19 ms on the caller's
// sidecar, 2.53-2.56 ms on the callee's two (dapr + ecs) per call and 2.68 ms per delivered event,
// at low rates (cases/misarch/timing/RESULTS.md §5), but at load (§4) the services' sidecars cost
// 16.6 ms per session and the gateway's 5.8, which these per-call values reproduce (AB32.3: 17.2
// and 6.1). The middle per hop (DESIGN.md AB39), one place: sidecar CPU callerCpuMs, calleeCpuMs,
// publishCpuMs, deliverCpuMs (measured, above); latencyMs (measured on one host, loopback);
// networkMs 0.1 ms, the one-way network delay of a message between pods on different nodes of one
// zone (ASSUMED, literature default, cases/misarch/timing/LITERATURE-NETWORK.md; not in the
// single-host measurements): a remote hop is two messages (a call's request and response; a
// message's sender -> broker -> subscriber), the publisher's wait the broker's acknowledgement
// round trip (+2 networkMs on Latency.publishWaitMs); 0 within a container (no hop); not on
// database accesses (every schema is remote from every cut alike; the measured statement round
// trip stays). The broker's delivery: the broker's CPU and the transit (measured), the publisher's
// wait (Latency, measured); core/tools/sensitivity.py scales networkMs. The sidecars' CPU only:
// the callee application's receiving envelope of a synchronous call across containers is its
// ReceivingEnvelope's, in its own container (DESIGN.md AB56).
remoteCalls : RemoteCalls {
    callerCpuMs  = 0.766
    calleeCpuMs  = 1.095
    latencyMs    = 0.2
    publishCpuMs = 0.434
    deliverCpuMs = 1.1
    sidecar      = true
    networkMs    = 0.1
}
